            <div xml:id="d30">

                <pb facs="0179" n="81" xml:id="pg81"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1053: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Perkins</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-01-09T15:00:00+08:00">January 9, 1930—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 9—9:05 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>28. Department’s 419, December 18, 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">7</hi>]
                        p.m.,<anchor n="49" xml:id="d30-f1"/> was repeated to the American Consul at
                    Shanghai with the additional statement made in the Department’s 434, December
                    30, 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">7</hi>] p.m.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d30-f2"/>
                    Consul General has replied as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 8, 5 p.m. I am very glad to receive for its guidance the clear
                            statement of policy in regard to the protection of public utilities in
                            Shanghai. The responsibility for the protection of public utilities
                            necessarily rests with the municipal police in the first instance but
                            when there existed fear that this should be inadequate in the past the
                            responsibility for protection of privately owned utilities has been
                            considered to be upon the forces of the nation concerned if available.
                            The policy enumerated in the Legation’s instruction is regarded as
                            indeed equitable, but I venture the suggestion that during periods when
                            conditions are tense the presence of an American or other foreign naval
                            vessel at an anchorage nearby would have a very excellent moral effect.
                            Though anchored at buoys frequently used it might even render
                            unnecessary display of great protective force by police.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
